      "latency_ms": 1833.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 736,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, enabling phenomena like capillary action.<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating bodies of water in winter. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 361 million km²). Found in oceans, rivers, lakes, glaciers, groundwater, and the atmosphere. |\n| **Biological role** | • Constitutes ~60 % of the human body (by weight).<br>• Acts as a medium for biochemical reactions, transport of nutrients, waste removal, temperature regulation, and structural support in cells (e.g., cytoplasm). |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | • Drinking water and food preparation.<br>• Agriculture (irrigation).<br>• Power generation (hydropower, steam turbines).<br>• Manufacturing (cooling, cleaning, solvents).<br>• Transportation (ships, pipelines). |\n| **Environmental importance** | • Drives the **hydrologic (water) cycle**: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → repeat.<br>• Regulates climate through heat transport and cloud formation.<br>• Supports ecosystems ranging from deep‑sea vents to desert oases. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n1. **Polarity** gives it strong hydrogen‑bonding, which underlies many of its unusual physical properties (high boiling point, surface tension, etc.).\n2. **Hydrogen bonding** creates a dynamic network that can rearrange quickly, allowing water to flow easily while still holding together as a liquid.\n3. **Versatility as a solvent** enables it to dissolve salts, sugars, gases, and many organic molecules, making it the medium in which most biological chemistry occurs.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) that can exist as ice, liquid, or vapor under Earth‑like conditions. Its exceptional thermal, mechanical, and solvent properties arise from hydrogen bonding, making it indispensable for climate regulation, geological processes, industrial applications, and all known forms of life."
